{"author":{"id":"29588b87a98184856c6eb9104921c34cca98a9cb029a2ee86cc10622d693ba86"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794789505,"nonce":"FoXo/LZuLRqUGpJB3a28qklfReg=","title":"rich_text_editor: spans in character or grapheme units, not byte offsets","message":"Low priority, not blocking anything. `TextSpan` (`styled_text.rs`) stores `start` and `end` as byte indices, and `document.rs` converts between columns and bytes at several call sites (`char_to_byte`, `byte_to_char`, `span_at_byte`, defined at `document.rs:303-324`). That is easy to get wrong with multi-byte text, and every caller has to know the convention.\n\nWould it be possible to store spans in grapheme (or at least character) units for the rich text editor, converting to bytes only at the rendering boundary? That would remove the conversions from the editing code, and it would avoid slicing at a non-boundary when a caller passes a column that is not a char boundary. If byte offsets must stay for `TextSpan`, documenting the invariant on the editor's types would also help. Thank you for Blinc, and no rush on this.","files":null}]}